---
/**
 * The same five models, on the same five-step task, marked two different ways.
 *
 * Every number here is one arithmetic step from the one above it: the per-step
 * line climbs by exactly sixteen points at each size (20, 36, 52, 68, 84), and
 * the whole-answer line is each of those raised to the fifth power, because the
 * answer only counts when all five steps survive. 0.20^5 = 0.03%, 0.36^5 =
 * 0.6%, 0.52^5 = 3.8%, 0.68^5 = 14.5%, 0.84^5 = 41.8% — rounded on the page to
 * 0, 1, 4, 15, 42, which is what the prose quotes. Change one and both must
 * change.
 *
 * That is the sceptical case about emergence drawn once, without deciding it.
 * The figure shows what a threshold in the marking does to a chart; it does not
 * claim that every reported emergent ability is made this way, and the unit is
 * explicit that the question is open.
 *
 * Nothing is carried by colour: the two series are told apart by marker shape
 * (circle against square), by the value printed at every point, and by a label
 * at the end of each line (hard rule 9).
 *
 * Decorative on its own, so `aria-hidden`: `Figure` supplies the accessible
 * name and the description.
 */
---

<svg viewBox="0 0 640 320" class="w-full" aria-hidden="true">
  <rect
    x="8"
    y="8"
    width="624"
    height="304"
    rx="8"
    class="fill-none stroke-rule-strong"
    stroke-width="1.25"></rect>

  <text
    x="24"
    y="32"
    class="fill-ink font-display"
    font-size="14"
    font-weight="700">The same five models, marked two ways</text
  >
  <text x="24" y="48" class="fill-ink-faint font-mono" font-size="9"
    >a task with five steps in it, where the answer only counts if all five are
    right</text
  >

  <text x="80" y="60" class="fill-ink-faint font-mono" font-size="9"
    >per cent right — of steps, or of whole answers</text
  >

  <g class="stroke-rule" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="2 3">
    <line x1="80" y1="70" x2="530" y2="70"></line>
    <line x1="80" y1="140" x2="530" y2="140"></line>
  </g>

  <g class="stroke-rule" stroke-width="1">
    <line x1="80" y1="64" x2="80" y2="210"></line>
    <line x1="80" y1="210" x2="530" y2="210"></line>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink-faint font-mono" font-size="8" text-anchor="end">
    <text x="74" y="73">100%</text>
    <text x="74" y="143">50%</text>
    <text x="74" y="213">0%</text>
  </g>

  <!-- Marked per step: a perfectly steady climb, sixteen points at a time. -->
  <polyline
    points="96,182 200,159.6 304,137.2 408,114.8 512,92.4"
    class="fill-none stroke-accent-2"
    stroke-width="2"></polyline>
  <g class="fill-accent-2">
    <circle cx="96" cy="182" r="4"></circle>
    <circle cx="200" cy="159.6" r="4"></circle>
    <circle cx="304" cy="137.2" r="4"></circle>
    <circle cx="408" cy="114.8" r="4"></circle>
    <circle cx="512" cy="92.4" r="4"></circle>
  </g>

  <!-- Marked whole: the same five models, each figure raised to the fifth. -->
  <polyline
    points="96,210 200,209.2 304,204.7 408,189.6 512,151.5"
    class="fill-none stroke-accent"
    stroke-width="2"></polyline>
  <g class="fill-paper stroke-accent" stroke-width="1.5">
    <rect x="92" y="206" width="8" height="8"></rect>
    <rect x="196" y="205.2" width="8" height="8"></rect>
    <rect x="300" y="200.7" width="8" height="8"></rect>
    <rect x="404" y="185.6" width="8" height="8"></rect>
    <rect x="508" y="147.5" width="8" height="8"></rect>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink-muted font-mono" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle">
    <text x="96" y="173">20%</text>
    <text x="200" y="151">36%</text>
    <text x="304" y="128">52%</text>
    <text x="408" y="106">68%</text>
    <text x="512" y="83">84%</text>
    <text x="96" y="202">0%</text>
    <text x="200" y="201">1%</text>
    <text x="304" y="197">4%</text>
    <text x="408" y="182">15%</text>
    <text x="512" y="144">42%</text>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink-muted font-mono" font-size="8">
    <text x="524" y="88">marked per step</text>
    <text x="524" y="100">partial credit</text>
    <text x="524" y="148">marked whole</text>
    <text x="524" y="160">all five, or nothing</text>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink-muted font-mono" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">
    <text x="96" y="226">×1</text>
    <text x="200" y="226">×10</text>
    <text x="304" y="226">×100</text>
    <text x="408" y="226">×1,000</text>
    <text x="512" y="226">×10,000</text>
  </g>
  <text x="80" y="242" class="fill-ink-faint font-mono" font-size="9"
    >model size — each step ten times the one before</text
  >

  <text
    x="24"
    y="266"
    class="fill-ink font-display"
    font-size="12"
    font-weight="700"
    >Same five models. Same answers. Two ways of counting.</text
  >
  <g class="fill-ink-muted font-mono" font-size="9">
    <text x="24" y="282"
      >One climbs by the same sixteen points at every size. The other reads 0%,
      1%, 4%, 15%, 42%.</text
    >
    <text x="24" y="296"
      >Which of those two is the ability arriving is exactly what is argued
      about.</text
    >
  </g>
</svg>
